---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionRule from '../../components/SectionRule.astro';
import ResearchReadout from '../../components/ResearchReadout.astro';
import MetricRail from '../../components/MetricRail.astro';
import { verdant } from '../../data/research.js';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';

const worlds = Array.from({ length: 11 }, (_, index) => `W${index}`);

const experimentFlow = [
  { label: 'DESIGN EVIDENCE' },
  { label: 'FREEZE v0.8' },
  { label: 'DEVELOPMENT_08', detail: '29,696 TASKS + 512 REPLAYS' },
  { label: '11 FROZEN SCREENS' },
  { label: 'AUDIT', detail: 'WITHHELD', closed: true },
];

const roleAblations = [
  { label: 'FULL FIVE-ROLE SLATE', value: 0.1596346085 },
  { label: 'REPLACE EASY WIN', value: 0.1512216445 },
  { label: 'PURE TOP FIVE', value: 0.150507093, emphasis: true },
  { label: 'REPLACE EXPLORATION', value: 0.1522520257 },
];
---

<Base
  title="VERDANT · FI99 R&D"
  description="A falsifiable recommendation engine for sparse preferences, and the v0.8 confirmation experiment that stopped before AUDIT."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 sm:py-16">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <SectionRule />
        <p class="case-kicker font-mono">2026 · RECOMMENDER SYSTEMS</p>
        <h1 data-split class="font-display">VERDANT</h1>
        <p class="case-deck">A falsifiable recommendation engine for sparse, subjective preferences.</p>
        <p class="case-status font-mono">PAUSED AFTER v0.8 CONFIRMATION</p>

        <blockquote>
          “We wanted something that could tell us we were wrong. (It did.)”
        </blockquote>

        <div class="case-copy intro-copy">
          <p>
            VERDANT started as an awkward problem inside Fallow, our hobby-discovery project. Most recommenders have plenty of behaviour to learn from. You watched this, bought that, saved five songs, abandoned three carts. Hobby discovery barely has any of that. The best recommendation might be something you've never tried, never searched for, and couldn't name if we asked.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the question we kept circling was how much a system can figure out from a handful of uncertain answers, without pretending those answers are the whole person.
          </p>
          <p>
            That question turned into VERDANT, a Bayesian recommendation engine. It keeps preferences, constraints, motivation, and friction separate, tracks how unsure it is about you, picks its next question based on what it still doesn't know, and scores activities with a structured utility model.
          </p>
          <p>
            By version 0.8 it had also picked up something we cared about more than another scoring function: a way to prove us wrong.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-readout">
        <ResearchReadout entry={verdant} headingLevel="p" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="cold-start-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">01 / METHOD</p>
        <div class="section-grid">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h2 id="cold-start-heading">The cold start is the point</h2>
            <p>
              Fallow asks you what kind of activity you might enjoy before it has years of your clicks to study, which kills the usual recommender shortcut. You can tell us you like structure, or want something social, or hate long commutes. You can't hand us a ranked list of the person you'll be in a year.
            </p>
            <p>VERDANT models that uncertainty instead of sanding it off.</p>
            <p>Its production representation uses 26 latent coordinates:</p>
          </div>

          <aside class="section-note font-mono">
            <span>RESEARCH FOR FALLOW</span>
            <span>SUBJECTIVE PREFERENCES</span>
            <span>SPARSE EVIDENCE</span>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="method-strip" data-reveal>
          <figure>
            <code>z = [p, r, m] ∈ R²⁶</code>
            <figcaption>26D LATENT STATE</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <code>q(z) = N(μ, Σ)</code>
            <figcaption>MAP + LAPLACE</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <code>Ucore = F · ∛(σ(E) · σ(A) · σ(I))</code>
            <figcaption>STRUCTURED UTILITY</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p class="method-label font-mono">ADAPTIVE QUESTIONS</p>
        </div>

        <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
          <p>
            where <code>p</code> carries preference state, <code>r</code> carries friction, and <code>m</code> carries motivation. The inference layer keeps a Gaussian approximation,
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>q(z) = N(μ, Σ)</code>
          </p>
          <p>
            and updates it from pairwise and structured assessment answers using MAP estimation and a Laplace approximation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Activities carry uncertain annotations too. We don't treat a hobby label as fact. The engine separates estimated fit from barriers, keeps hard feasibility apart from soft friction, and lets your context change without quietly rewriting what you like long-term.
          </p>
          <p>For v0.8 we swapped the core score from a rank-based construction to a cardinal one:</p>
          <p><code>Ucore = F · ∛(σ(E) · σ(A) · σ(I))</code></p>
          <p>
            The three terms are the engine's modelled enjoyment, adoption, and impact channels, and <code>F</code> applies feasibility. We picked a geometric form on purpose: if one channel is weak, strength in the other two can't paper over it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="simulator-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">02 / SYNTHETIC LAB</p>
        <div class="section-grid">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h2 id="simulator-heading">We built a simulator because we didn't have the right human data</h2>
            <p>There's an easy way to make a recommendation model look impressive: invent a benchmark that likes it.</p>
            <p>We wanted ours to do the opposite (and be as difficult about it as possible).</p>
            <p>
              VERDANT's synthetic lab has 600 activities, 12 activity families, seven simulated annotators, a 260-question bank, and eleven worlds that each break a different assumption. Some add noise. Some shift preferences mid-run. Others corrupt answers, and a few go straight after the assumptions behind the activity model itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              To be clear, synthetic users aren't evidence that VERDANT understands real people. They answer narrower questions. Does the algorithm do what we specified? Does a component help when we know the ground truth? And does that help survive when we deliberately get the world wrong?
            </p>
            <p>
              We wrote that line into the project rules before any results came in. Synthetic results could tune software and check that we implemented the science correctly. They couldn't become claims about human psychology.
            </p>
          </div>

          <aside class="simulation-ledger font-mono" aria-label="Synthetic laboratory inventory" data-reveal>
            <div><strong>600</strong><span>ACTIVITIES</span></div>
            <div><strong>12</strong><span>ACTIVITY FAMILIES</span></div>
            <div><strong>07</strong><span>SIMULATED ANNOTATORS</span></div>
            <div><strong>260</strong><span>QUESTION BANK</span></div>
            <div><strong>11</strong><span>WORLDS</span></div>
            <p>SYNTHETIC SIMULATION ≠ EMPIRICAL VALIDATION</p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="frozen-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">03 / PREREGISTRATION</p>
        <div class="case-copy">
          <h2 id="frozen-heading">We froze the test before we saw the answer</h2>
          <p>
            The first seven gates built the simulator, baselines, inference machinery, recommendation policy, metrics, a deterministic random-number scheme, and the validity rules. Version 0.8 then placed a small number of explicit scientific bets.
          </p>
          <p>We froze the code at:</p>
          <p class="commit-ref font-mono">C_DEV_08 = 59ebb9bf5240ff5934c1bd9bfe157915af3f3c02</p>
          <p>Then we opened a fresh development namespace that none of the implementation work had touched.</p>
          <p>The run executed:</p>
          <ul class="run-ledger">
            <li><strong>29,696</strong> scientific tasks</li>
            <li><strong>512</strong> deterministic stopped-policy replays</li>
            <li><strong>11</strong> preregistered confirmation screens</li>
            <li><strong>0</strong> execution failures</li>
            <li><strong>0</strong> validity failures</li>
            <li><strong>0</strong> nonfinite scientific values</li>
            <li><strong>0</strong> hard-feasibility violations</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The scientific runner took about <strong>7 hours 46 minutes</strong> on four local workers.</p>
          <p>
            Behind the confirmation screens sat a separate AUDIT namespace. We'd only let ourselves open it if all eleven conditions passed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="experiment-flow" data-reveal>
          <figcaption class="font-mono">EXPERIMENT FLOW / FROZEN ORDER</figcaption>
          <ol>
            {
              experimentFlow.map((step) => (
                <li class:list={[step.closed && 'is-closed']}>
                  <strong>{step.label}</strong>
                  {step.detail && <span>{step.detail}</span>}
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section screen-section" aria-labelledby="screens-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">04 / CONFIRMATION</p>
        <div class="screen-heading">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h2 id="screens-heading">Five passed. Six didn't.</h2>
            <p>
              And there was no implementation excuse to hide behind. SCREEN-11 (integrity) passed cleanly, so this wasn't a corrupted run dressed up in scientific language.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="screen-total font-display" aria-label="Five screens passed and six screens failed">
            <span>05 PASS</span>
            <span>06 FAIL</span>
          </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="screen-matrix" aria-label="All eleven confirmation screens" data-reveal>
          {
            verdant.screens.map((screen) => (
              <li class:list={[screen.state === 'PASS' ? 'is-pass' : 'is-fail']}>
                <span class="screen-id font-mono">{screen.id}</span>
                <strong>{screen.name}</strong>
                <span class="screen-state font-mono">{screen.state}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <div class="result-section">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h3>The cardinal score survived</h3>
            <p>Our clearest win came from M1.</p>
            <p>On W0, lower regret is better:</p>
          </div>

          <figure class="result-figure m1-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="figure-header font-mono">
              <span>M1 / MEAN R@5</span>
              <span>LOWER IS BETTER</span>
            </figcaption>
            <MetricRail label="CARDINAL CORE" value={0.1222066422} max={0.1372602637} display="0.1222066422" emphasis />
            <MetricRail label="HISTORICAL RANK CORE" value={0.1372602637} max={0.1372602637} display="0.1372602637" />
            <ol class="world-strip" aria-label="Cardinal scoring result in worlds W0 through W10">
              {worlds.map((world) => <li><strong>{world}</strong><span>CARDINAL BETTER</span></li>)}
            </ol>
          </figure>

          <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
            <p>
              The paired mean difference was <code>-0.0150536215</code>, roughly an 11% reduction from the historical rank formulation.
            </p>
            <p>
              More importantly, the direction held in every simulated world from W0 to W10. Cardinal scoring beat rank scoring <strong>11 out of 11</strong> times.
            </p>
            <p>
              We changed one of VERDANT's most basic assumptions, and the new version held up in every misspecification world in the confirmation set. We're keeping it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="result-section">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h3>Our preferred question selector lost</h3>
            <p>We expected raw information gain to beat the adjusted selector we'd used before. Fresh evidence disagreed.</p>
          </div>

          <figure class="result-figure selector-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="figure-header font-mono">
              <span>M2 / SELECTOR</span>
              <span>LOWER IS BETTER</span>
            </figcaption>
            <div class="selector-grid">
              <section aria-labelledby="raw-ig-heading">
                <h4 id="raw-ig-heading" class="font-mono">RAW_IG</h4>
                <dl class="font-mono">
                  <div><dt>AUR</dt><dd>0.1359898122</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Q_EQUIV</dt><dd>7</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </section>
              <section class="is-preferred" aria-labelledby="adjusted-ig-heading">
                <h4 id="adjusted-ig-heading" class="font-mono">ADJUSTED_IG_08</h4>
                <dl class="font-mono">
                  <div><dt>AUR</dt><dd>0.1290738506</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Q_EQUIV</dt><dd>6</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </section>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
            <p>Adjusted IG reached the fixed F2 reference one question sooner, with lower area-under-regret.</p>
            <p>SCREEN-2 and SCREEN-3 failed for the same reason: we backed the wrong selector.</p>
            <p>Nothing was broken. We were just wrong, which is the whole reason you run the thing in the first place.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="result-section">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h3>Five questions was too eager</h3>
            <p>The frozen stopping search picked:</p>
            <p class="thresholds font-mono">
              τregret = 0.158403035926751<br />
              τdecision = 0.02187763557997514
            </p>
            <p>The median user stopped after <strong>5 questions</strong>.</p>
            <p>That sounds efficient until you look at what it did to recommendation regret:</p>
          </div>

          <figure class="result-figure stopping-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="figure-header font-mono">
              <span>STOPPING / W0 POLICY</span>
              <span>LOWER IS BETTER</span>
            </figcaption>
            <div class="stopping-grid">
              <p class="question-count"><strong>05</strong><span>QUESTIONS</span></p>
              <div>
                <MetricRail label="F0 ORACLE BASELINE" value={0.1264057862} max={0.1596346085} display="0.1264057862" emphasis />
                <MetricRail label="F2 @ 13 QUESTIONS" value={0.1506734973} max={0.1596346085} display="0.1506734973" />
                <MetricRail label="STOPPED F3" value={0.1596346085} max={0.1596346085} display="0.1596346085" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
            <p>
              SCREEN-4 needed stopped F3 to beat a limit of <code>0.1200854969</code>. It missed, by a lot. The stopping selector did exactly what we told it to. The problem was the objective we froze, which let a policy trade away too much recommendation quality for fewer questions.
            </p>
            <p>
              And it wasn't only W0. Across the eight Gate-E misspecification worlds, F3 beat F0 in <strong>0 of 8</strong>. Median F3 regret was <code>0.1606971848</code>, versus <code>0.1528195242</code> for F2.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="result-section">
          <div class="case-copy">
            <h3>The five-role slate was expensive</h3>
            <p>
              VERDANT's slate policy tried to do more than hand you the five highest-utility activities. It gave each recommendation a job: an Easy Win, a Serendipity pick, an Exploration pick, and so on.
            </p>
            <p>Fresh ablations showed what that cost us.</p>
          </div>

          <figure class="result-figure role-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="figure-header font-mono">
              <span>ROLE ABLATIONS / MEAN R@5</span>
              <span>LOWER IS BETTER</span>
            </figcaption>
            <div class="role-grid">
              <div>
                {
                  roleAblations.map((row) => (
                    <MetricRail
                      label={row.label}
                      value={row.value}
                      max={0.1596346085}
                      display={row.value.toFixed(10)}
                      emphasis={row.emphasis}
                    />
                  ))
                }
              </div>
              <dl class="role-ledger font-mono">
                <div><dt>D5 USERS</dt><dd>512 / 512</dd></div>
                <div><dt>REDUNDANCY</dt><dd>0.000</dd></div>
                <div><dt>UTILITY RETENTION</dt><dd>94.47%</dd></div>
                <div><dt>REQUIRED</dt><dd>97%</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
            <p>Every replacement we tested improved regret.</p>
            <p>
              D5 adds another angle. All 512 W0 users had at least five primary-eligible activities, and mean redundancy was <code>0.0</code>, so the slate really was diverse. It still kept only about <strong>94.47%</strong> of the pure-top-five comparator's true utility, under the frozen 97% bar.
            </p>
            <p>
              So we managed to make the slate interesting, and failed to show that interesting was worth paying for.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="survivors-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">05 / SURVIVORS</p>
        <div class="case-copy">
          <h2 id="survivors-heading">Two unusual ideas did survive</h2>
          <p>The drift test passed, and for a reason the old metric would've missed.</p>
          <p>After a simulated preference shock:</p>
        </div>

        <div class="survivor-grid">
          <figure class="drift-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="figure-header font-mono">
              <span>DRIFT / W0</span>
              <span>LOWER IS BETTER</span>
            </figcaption>
            <ol class="drift-points">
              <li><span class="font-mono">B_PRE</span><strong>0.1596346085</strong></li>
              <li><span class="font-mono">L0 / SHOCK</span><strong>0.1680195819</strong></li>
              <li><span class="font-mono">L10 / RECOVERY</span><strong>0.1093558435</strong></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="drift-line" aria-hidden="true">
              <span></span><span></span><span></span>
            </div>
            <dl class="drift-ledger font-mono">
              <div><dt>DAMAGE RECOVERY</dt><dd>6.996×</dd></div>
              <div><dt>LEGACY RATIO</dt><dd>0.349</dd></div>
              <div><dt>LEGACY</dt><dd>REPORTING ONLY</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </figure>

          <figure class="serendipity-figure" data-reveal>
            <figcaption class="font-mono">SERENDIPITY / NARROW PASS</figcaption>
            <p><strong>410</strong><span>/ 512</span></p>
            <dl class="font-mono">
              <div><dt>OBSERVED</dt><dd>80.078%</dd></div>
              <div><dt>THRESHOLD</dt><dd>80%</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="case-copy continuation-copy">
          <p>
            The shock did <code>0.0083849734</code> of measured damage. Ten new answers recovered far more than that, for an uncapped damage-recovery value of <code>6.9963</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The older reporting ratio was only <code>0.3491</code>. Version 0.8 had frozen the corrected, damage-based definition before the run, and that's the one that passed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Serendipity passed too, by about the narrowest margin possible at this population size: <strong>410 of 512</strong> eligible users succeeded, or <code>0.80078125</code>, against a threshold of <code>0.80</code>.
          </p>
          <p>One fewer user and it fails. We wouldn't have moved either threshold if the number had landed on the other side, so a pass here means the frozen rule passed and nothing more.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section audit-section" aria-labelledby="audit-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">06 / CLOSED GATE</p>
        <div class="case-copy">
          <h2 id="audit-heading">So we didn't run AUDIT</h2>
          <p>Version 0.8 failed six of eleven confirmation screens.</p>
          <p>
            Our frozen rule said AUDIT could only run if every screen passed and the integrity checks were clean. The integrity checks were clean. The science wasn't.
          </p>
          <p>We left AUDIT untouched.</p>
          <p>
            Honestly, that decision matters more to us than any flattering way we could've summarized the scorecard. We split design evidence, confirmation evidence, and a held-out audit for one reason: so we couldn't treat every disappointing result as permission for one more tweak.
          </p>
          <p>
            The DEVELOPMENT_08 realization is design evidence now. We won't rerun it as a fresh confirmation attempt, and we won't retune 0.8 against it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="audit-gate font-mono" aria-label="AUDIT was not run" data-reveal>
          <div class="audit-title">
            <strong>AUDIT</strong>
            <span>GATE CLOSED</span>
          </div>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>STATUS</dt><dd>NOT RUN</dd></div>
            <div><dt>NAMESPACE</dt><dd>UNTOUCHED</dd></div>
            <div><dt>AUTHORIZATION</dt><dd>REFUSED</dd></div>
            <div><dt>REASON</dt><dd>6 / 11 SCREENS FAILED</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <div class="gate-bars" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section final-section" aria-labelledby="state-heading">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <p class="section-index font-mono">07 / CURRENT STATE</p>
        <div class="case-copy">
          <h2 id="state-heading">Current state</h2>
          <p><strong>VERDANT is paused after v0.8 confirmation.</strong></p>
          <p>
            We're keeping the parts that earned our confidence, mainly cardinal scoring and the discipline around uncertainty, drift, validity, and reproducibility. The confirmation run also gave us real reasons to doubt raw information gain, aggressive stopping, and the current five-role slate.
          </p>
          <p>Fallow can use those lessons without pretending VERDANT 0.8 graduated.</p>
          <p>
            We do expect to come back to this. A future version would start as a new hypothesis, with a new preregistration and a fresh development namespace, instead of a patched reading of this result.
          </p>
          <p>For now, the experiment is done.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="final-readout font-mono" data-reveal>
          <p>PAUSED AFTER v0.8 CONFIRMATION</p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>C_DEV_08</dt><dd>59ebb9bf…</dd></div>
            <div><dt>SUMMARY</dt><dd>503c3d6e…</dd></div>
            <div><dt>AUDIT</dt><dd>UNTOUCHED</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
